course: dictionary
lesson: 10029
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word tension has more than one meaning, so context matters. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer wants. «Water molecules pull tightly on each other at the surface, making surface tension.» Here, pulling and surface tell you it is a science word about a force.
explain: In a story, tension is the exciting feeling that something is about to happen. Writers build tension so that readers want to read on. Reading like a writer means noticing how an author builds tension, not just what happens next.
explain: Tension can also be a feeling of worry or nervousness, especially when people are not getting on. A room with tension in the air feels uncomfortable, and people may be stressed. The words around the word show you which feeling it is.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If worry, excitement, pulling force or tightness fits and still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Tension is a noun. Its plural, tensions, is often used for quarrels or worries between people.
fact: In science, tension is the pulling force in a rope, string or wire when it is stretched tight.
fact: Surface tension is the tightness at the top of water, which is why pond skaters can walk on a pond.
fact: Tension can also mean tightness, like a tight muscle.
fact: Tension is spelled with -sion because its root is tense, and roots ending in d, de, se or nd usually take -sion.
exercise: «Pond skaters walk on the surface tension of the water.» What does tension mean here? | A loud noise | Worry about getting wet | Excitement about a story | *A pulling force at the top of the water
exercise: «Writers build tension so that readers want to read on.» What does tension mean here? | Tightness in a muscle | *The exciting feeling that something is about to happen | A rope pulled tight | Nervousness between friends
exercise: Which spelling is correct, because its root is tense? | tenshun | *tension | tention | tenssion
test: «There was tension between the two friends after the argument.» What does tension mean here? | *Worry and nervousness between people | A rope pulled tight | A film of tightness on the water | An exciting part of a story
test: In a science lesson, what is the pulling force in a rope or string that is stretched tight called? | Tiredness | Friction | Mood | *Tension
exam: «Her shoulders were full of tension after the long day.» What does tension mean here? | A quarrel between friends | *Tightness, like in a muscle | A pulling force in a rope | The exciting feeling of a story
